<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Jayshani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 915</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Jayshani (died 915) was an Isma'ili missionary (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai">da'i</a>) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Sent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a> and arriving around 881, he built a following in the south of the country, seized Sanaa in 905 and conquered much of Yemen, but then broke with the future Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and claimed authority for himself. He died, reportedly poisoned, in 915, and his state collapsed soon after.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali ibn al-Fadl was born in the village of Suhayb, near the town of Jayshan in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, now ruined, near modern Qa'tabah. A Shi'ite of the Sabaean tribe, he went on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 880 with others of his tribe and travelled on to the shrine of Karbala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. There an Isma'ili agent noticed his fervent devotion before al-Husayn's tomb and recruited him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> was then in crisis after the disappearance of the eleventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, Hasan al-Askari, in 874, and many dissatisfied Twelvers turned to the Isma'ili preaching of the imminent return of the mahdī. Ali set out with an older convert, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, in late May or early June 881, travelling anonymously with the pilgrim caravans, and the two reached northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was divided between the Yu'firid dynasty, which held much of the interior, the Ziyadids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a>, the Banu Manakh around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Taiz</a> and tribes owing allegiance to no one. Ali returned to his home region, preached in the mountains of Jebel Yafi'i, won the support of the ruler of Mudhaykhira and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Lahj. On 25 January 905 he evicted his former ally from Mudhaykhira, and after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> in November 905 he campaigned across the country, taking al-Mahjam, al-Kadra and, briefly, Zabid in 906.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> changed hands several times before he took it for good in August 911. He then renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a>, who had established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 909, and declared himself the mahdī. The reason is unknown; it may have been personal ambition or disillusionment, and a letter from al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> setting out his descent and denying that his rule would bring the end times had caused much unease. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a> refused to follow him, Ali blockaded him in the Jabal Maswar until he sought terms in April 912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ali died at Mudhaykhira on 28 October 915 of an unknown illness; later Isma'ili legend claimed that agents of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> posing as physicians had poisoned him. The Yu'firid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asad-ibn-ibrahim">As'ad ibn Ibrahim</a>, who had been left in control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a>, began retaking the fortresses in January 916, and on 6 January 917 his forces stormed Mudhaykhira and took Ali's two sons and three daughters prisoner. As'ad executed the two sons and sent their heads to the Abbasid caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Ibn Hawshab's northern community, by contrast, has survived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Jayshani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They faced the arrival of the Zaydi imam al-Hadi in the north in 897 and the Isma'ili missionaries <b>Ali ibn al-Fadl</b> and Ibn Hawshab, who briefly captured Sanaa.</blockquote><a href="/state/yufirid-dynasty">Yufirid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
